" (https://apps.fas.usda.gov/psdonline/circulars/sugar.pdf)\nciteturn27view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 16; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn26view0\",\"lineno\":89}); Total lines: 581\nL73@P2: highlighting the reduced incentive for imports at present.\nL74@P2: With softer incentives and strict controls, China sugar imports will likely remain subdued in the year\nL75@P2: ahead.\nL76@P2: 6 Source: Hi Sugar Tech, Estimated Costs and Profits of Sugar Imports, available at hisugar.com\nL77@P2: 0\nL78@P2: 1\nL79@P2: 2\nL80@P2: 3\nL81@P2: 4\nL82@P2: 5\nL83@P2: 6\nL84@P2: 2021/2022 2022/2023 2023/2024 2024/2025 2025/2026 2026/27\nL85@P2: Milion Tons\nL86@P2: China Sugar Imports By Partner (Oct/Sep)\nL87@P2: Brazil South Korea El Salvador Thailand Others Forecast\nL88@P2: Foreign Agricultural Service/USDA 3 November 2026\nL89@P2-3: Global Market AnalysisGlobal production is forecast at 178.1 million metric tons raw value (tons), about 5 percent lower\nL90@P3: than the revised 2025/26 estimate. Smaller sugar production in India, the European Union, the\nL91@P3: Philippines, and Australia offset larger crops in China and Russia.\nL92@P3: Consumption is forecast at 176.4 million tons, almost unchanged from 2025/26. Larger\nL93@P3: consumption in India, Brazil, Turkey, and Pakistan is expected to drive world consumption higher,\nL94@P3: even as use declines in Indonesia, Algeria, and Iraq.\nL95@P3: Exports are forecast down 5 percent to 61.5 million tons. Better prospects for Australia and\nL96@P3: Thailand are more than offset by lower exports from Brazil, the European Union, and India. Imports\nL97@P3: are down 4 percent to 55.2 million tons, as weaker imports for the UAE, China, and India offset\nL98@P3: larger volumes for the United States, Saudi Arabia, and Turkey.\nL99@P3: With growth in consumption outpacing production, global ending stocks are forecast 12 percent\nL100@P3: lower than in 2025/26 to 39.8 million tons, the lowest since 2011/12. Significant drawdowns in\nL101@P3: India, Thailand, the European Union, and Indonesia support stable consumption. In contrast, large\nL102@P3: supplies and weak demand have significantly raised ending stocks in China.\nL103@P3: 2026/27 Global Sugar Outlook\nL104@P3: 0\nL105@P3: 20\nL106@P3: 40\nL107@P3: 60\nL108@P3: 80\nL109@P3: 100\nL110@P3: 120\nL111@P3: 140\nL112@P3: 160\nL113@P3: 180\nL114@P3: 200\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://apps.fas.usda.gov/psdonline/circulars/sugar.pdf)\nciteturn27view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 16; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn26view0\",\"pattern\":\"China\"}); Total lines: 581\nL0@P0: United States Department of Agriculture Foreign Agricultural Service\nL1@P0: November 2026 Sugar: World Markets and Trade\nL2@P0: Rising China sugar production has outpaced growth in consumer demand, dramatically reducing\nL3@P0: the need for imports. With ample stocks, the forecast for sugar imports is revised down to about 4.2\nL4@P0: million metric tons, raw value (tons) in 2025/26 and even lower in 2026/27 to 3.6 million tons.\nL5@P0: China’s sugar deficit is estimated at 2.4 million tons in 2025/26, 42 percent lower than in 2024/25\nL6@P0: and the lowest figure in a decade, with a further decline forecast in 2026/27.\nL7@P0: While China remains one of the largest consumers of sugar in the world in absolute terms,\nL8@P0: consumption growth has been easing. Between 2020 and 2024, per capita sugar consumption in\nL9@P0: China grew less than 2 percent to about 11.1 kg/person, well below the world average of 4 percent\nL10@P0: growth and 22.3 kg/person in the same period1\nL11@P0: . While China has long lagged global peers due to a\nL12@P0: lower cultural preference for sweeteners, slowing growth is increasingly attributed to a shift in\nL13@P0: consumer attitudes towards sugar. Reporting from FAS/Beijing indicates that consumers are\nL14@P0: increasingly preferring low-sugar or sugar-free beverages2\nL15@P0: , with a similar trend in baking and\nL16@P0: confectionary goods3\nL17@P0: . These trends are curtailing upstream demand for sugar.\nL18@P0: 1 Japan Sugar Refiners Association citing ISO, Per capita sugar consumption in major countries, 2020 and 2024 editions.\nL19@P0: 2024 statistics available at https://www.seitokogyokai.com/statistics/pdf/statistics02.pdf, 2 Unlocking Opportunities in Chinas 170 billion Dollar Beverage Market, USDA GAIN CH2025-0122 3 Sweet Success Unlocking Opportunities in China Evolving Confectionery Market, USDA GAIN CH2025-0201\nL20@P0: 0\nL21@P0: 2\nL22@P0: 4\nL23@P0: 6\nL24@P0: 8\nL25@P0: 10\nL26@P0: 12\nL27@P0: 14\nL28@P0: 16\nL29@P0: 2016/17 2017/18 2018/19 2019/20 2020/21 2021/22 2022/23 2023/24 2024/25 2025/26 2026/27\nL30@P0: Million Tons\nL31@P0: China Sugar Production, Imports, Consumption (Oct/Sep)\nL32@P0: Imports Production Consumption\nL33@P0-1: China Sugar Deficit Falls to 10-Year Low, Curbing ImportsSource: Hi Sugar Tech, Estimated Costs and Profits of Sugar Imports, available at hisugar.com\nL34@P1: Despite easing demand, domestic sugar production soared over the same period. Centrifugal sugar\nL35@P1: production climbed by over 20 percent since 2020/21 to 12.9 million tons in 2025/26, with 75\nL36@P1: percent of that growth coming from higher cane sugar production. Financial support from large\nL37@P1: sugar mills and provincial governments4 for cane sugar production in Guanxi and Yunnan continues\nL38@P1: to result in high producer prices for sugar relative to other fruit and tropical crops in the same\nL39@P1: growing regions. Price pressure from global supplies is also limited. Imports of sugar are tightly\nL40@P1: controlled, with China applying a 15-percent ad valorem tariff on sugar imports within a 1.945\nL41@P1: million tons tariff rate quota (TRQ), which climbs to 50 percent for out-of-quota volume. Both in\u0002and out-of-quota volumes are also subject to a value-added tax of 13 percent. Allocation of the\nL42@P1: quota is also controlled tightly, further marginalizing imports.\nL43@P1: With crop production greatly outpacing demand, stocks are expected to swell, with 2025/26 ending\nL44@P1: stocks forecast at 3.2 million tons, almost doubling year over year and expected to rise to 4.5\nL45@P1: million tons in 2026/27. Prices support larger stockpiles and weaker consumption. Spot prices for\nL46@P1: white (refined) No. 1 Granulated Sugar5 have fallen 8 percent from year ago levels, while nearby\nL47@P1: Zhengzhou future’s contracts have fallen about 5 percent – both signaling large supply pressure.\nL48@P1: This is in stark contrast to global prices; the ICE No. 11 contract is up by more than 20 percent\nL49@P1: during the same period.\nL50@P1: 4 See Sugar Annual - 2026, GAIN- CH2026-0051 from FAS/Guangzhou for specific details 5 SunSirs--China Commodity Data Group, Spot Prices for grade 1 white granulated sugar, available at:\nL51@P1: https://www.sunsirs.com/uk/sdetail.html. Nearby Zhengzhou Sugar Futures are available at: hisugar.com\nL52@P1: 3,000\nL53@P1: 3,750\nL54@P1: 4,500\nL55@P1: 5,250\nL56@P1: 6,000\nL57@P1: 6,750\nL58@P1: RMB/ton\nL59@P1: China Import vs. Domestic Spot Sugar Prices\nL60@P1: In-quota import price (RMB/t) Out-of-quota import price (RMB/t) Domestic spot price\nL61@P1: Foreign Agricultural Service/USDA 2 November 2026\nL62@P1-2: Global Market AnalysisSource: Trade Data Monitor, LLC.\nL63@P2: Similar issues are expected to persist in 2026/27. Just 6 months ago, world sugar prices were facing\nL64@P2: multi-year lows, but supply shocks are now reversing this trend and pushing prices back up to\nL65@P2: levels last seen in 2023. World sugar prices have continued to climb higher since the May 2026\nL66@P2: report as a El Nino driven weather phenomenon including excess precipitation in Brazil and\nL67@P2: droughts in India and Thailand have reduced world sugar supplies and exports. As such, while in\nL68@P2: quota import margins remain positive, out of quota import margins are turning negative. HiSugar, a\nL69@P2: China sugar price analysis firm, estimates a more than $80/ton loss on out-of-quota imports of\nL70@P2: Brazilian raw sugar against domestic supplies as of October 12, 2026. This stands in sharp contrast\nL71@P2: to a year ago, when imports generated a profit of more than $130/ton on October 30, 20256\nL72@P2: ,\nL73@P2: highlighting the reduced incentive for imports at present.\nL74@P2: With softer incentives and strict controls, China sugar imports will likely remain subdued in the year\nL75@P2: ahead.\nL76@P2: 6 Source: Hi Sugar Tech, Estimated Costs and Profits of Sugar Imports, available at hisugar.com\nL77@P2: 0\nL78@P2: 1\nL79@P2: 2\nL80@P2: 3\nL81@P2: 4\nL82@P2: 5\nL83@P2: 6\nL84@P2: 2021/2022 2022/2023 2023/2024 2024/2025 2025/2026 2026/27\nL85@P2: Milion Tons\nL86@P2: China Sugar Imports By Partner (Oct/Sep)\nL87@P2: Brazil South Korea El Salvador Thailand Others Forecast\nL88@P2: Foreign Agricultural Service/USDA 3 November 2026\nL89@P2-3: Global Market AnalysisGlobal production is forecast at 178.1 million metric tons raw value (tons), about 5 percent lower\nL90@P3: than the revised 2025/26 estimate. Smaller sugar production in India, the European Union, the\nL91@P3: Philippines, and Australia offset larger crops in China and Russia.\nL92@P3: Consumption is forecast at 176.4 million tons, almost unchanged from 2025/26. Larger\nL93@P3: consumption in India, Brazil, Turkey, and Pakistan is expected to drive world consumption higher,\nL94@P3: even as use declines in Indonesia, Algeria, and Iraq.\nL95@P3: Exports are forecast down 5 percent to 61.5 million tons. Better prospects for Australia and\nL96@P3: Thailand are more than offset by lower exports from Brazil, the European Union, and India. Imports\nL97@P3: are down 4 percent to 55.2 million tons, as weaker imports for the UAE, China, and India offset\nL98@P3: larger volumes for the United States, Saudi Arabia, and Turkey.\nL99@P3: With growth in consumption outpacing production, global ending stocks are forecast 12 percent\nL100@P3: lower than in 2025/26 to 39.8 million tons, the lowest since 2011/12. Significant drawdowns in\nL101@P3: India, Thailand, the European Union, and Indonesia support stable consumption. In contrast, large\nL102@P3: supplies and weak demand have significantly raised ending stocks in China.\nL103@P3: 2026/27 Global Sugar Outlook\nL104@P3: 0\nL105@P3: 20\nL106@P3: 40\nL107@P3: 60\nL108@P3: 80\nL109@P3: 100\nL110@P3: 120\nL111@P3: 140\nL112@P3: 160\nL113@P3: 180\nL114@P3: 200\nL115@P3: 2022/23 2023/24 2024/25 2025/26 2026/27\nL116@P3: Million Tons\nL117@P3: 2026/2027 Global Sugar Production Down 5 Percent\nL118@P3: Brazil India China European Union Thailand ROW\nL119@P3: Foreign Agricultural Service/USDA 4 November 2026\nL120@P3-4: Global Market Analysis2026/27 India sugar production is now forecast at 29.5 million tons, 500,000 tons lower than in\nL121@P4: 2025/26. Weak rainfall during India’s southwest monsoon season resulted in a smaller sugar cane\nL122@P4: harvest and weaker sugar recovery from stalks, shrinking total sugar production. Still, India sugar\nL123@P4: consumption is expected to increase by 2 percent to 30.8 million tons in 2026/27. Growing\nL124@P4: industrial demand remains the largest driver of use as increases in urbanization and income\nL125@P4: continue to encourage higher consumption of items such as soft drinks and baked goods7\nL126@P4: . Imports\nL127@P4: of 3.0 million tons, including 1.0 million tons of duty-free sugar imports under tariff-rate quota, are\nL128@P4: expected to support consumption. However, use is increasingly facilitated by a drawdown of\nL129@P4: stocks, forecast down to 4.3 million tons, the lowest level in 20 years. Exports are expected to fall\nL130@P4: 13 percent to 3.1 million tons – the lowest in a decade – due to lower supplies.\nL131@P4: 7 See FAS GAIN report India Sugar Semi-Annual, October 2026, p5 for a more detailed breakdown.\nL132@P4: 0\nL133@P4: 2\nL134@P4: 4\nL135@P4: 6\nL136@P4: 8\nL137@P4: 10\nL138@P4: 12\nL139@P4: 0\nL140@P4: 5\nL141@P4: 10\nL142@P4: 15\nL143@P4: 20\nL144@P4: 25\nL145@P4: 30\nL146@P4: 35\nL147@P4: 40\nL148@P4: 2022/23 2023/24 2024/25 2025/26 2026/27\nL149@P4: Million TonsMillion Tons\nL150@P4: India Sugar Production, Consumption and Ending Stocks (Oct/Sep)\nL151@P4: Production (left axis) Consumption (left axis) Ending Stocks (right axis)\nL152@P4: 2026/27 Country Specific Sugar Outlooks\nL153@P4: Foreign Agricultural Service/USDA 5 November 2026\nL154@P4-5: Global Market AnalysisSource: Brazil Ministério da Agricultura e Pecuária (MAPA), Monitoring of Sugar and Ethanol Production\nL155@P5: Production in Brazil is forecast 6 percent lower to 40.5 million tons, a 3-million-ton reduction from\nL156@P5: 2025/26. Heavy rains in Brazil’s center-south has lowered total sugar recovery and impacted cane\nL157@P5: quality, resulting in more sugar from the current crop diverted towards ethanol use. With tighter\nL158@P5: supplies, exports – which represent more than 70 percent of use in most years – are forecast to fall\nL159@P5: to 31.7 million tons, about 7 percent below 2025/26. Lower production is also expected to temper\nL160@P5: domestic availability, resulting in consumption dropping 7 percent to 8.8 million tons.\nL247@P7: other sweeteners . Exports are forecast stable at 1.2 million tons with a much higher proportion of\nL248@P7: exports forecast to head to the United States than in 2025/26.\nL249@P7: 0\nL250@P7: 2\nL251@P7: 4\nL252@P7: 6\nL253@P7: 8\nL254@P7: 10\nL255@P7: 12\nL256@P7: 14\nL257@P7: 2022/2023 2023/2024 2024/2025 2025/2026 2026/2027 (Nov)\nL258@P7: Million Tons\nL259@P7: China Sugar Production and Imports\nL260@P7: Beet Sugar Cane Sugar Imports\nL261@P7: Foreign Agricultural Service/USDA 8 November 2026\nL262@P7-8: Global Market Analysis2026/27 Updated Trade Outlook\nL263@P8: Major Exporters:\nL264@P8: 2025/26 2026/27 Change Reason\nL265@P8: WORLD 64,756 61,489 -3,267\nL266@P8: Brazil 34,052 31,721 -2,331 Weaker sugar production to\nL267@P8: date\nL268@P8: Thailand 6,200 7,000 800 Weaker competitor exports\nL269@P8: India 3,550 3,100 -450 Smaller crop reducing raw\nL270@P8: exports\nL271@P8: EU-27 1,988 608 -1,380 Smaller crop and suspension\nL272@P8: of inward processing regime\nL273@P8: Major Importers:\nL274@P8: 2025/26 2026/27 Change Reason\nL275@P8: WORLD 57,637 55,219 -2,418\nL276@P8: Indonesia 3,991 3,900 -91 Policy restrictions on imports\nL277@P8: China 4,296 3,600 -696 Ample domestic supplies\nL278@P8: United States 2,778 3,112 334 Larger imports from Mexico\nL279@P8: EU-27 1,880 2,250 370 Smaller domestic crop\nL280@P8: India 3,330 3,030 -300 Duty-free TRQ, smaller crop\nL281@P8: Foreign Agricultural Service/USDA 9 November 2026\nL282@P8-9: Global Market AnalysisGlobal sugar production in 2025/26 is revised up about 1 percent to 187.1 million tons from the\nL283@P9: May 2026 forecast, as increases for the European Union, China, Thailand, and Guatemala more\nL284@P9: than offset smaller estimates for Brazil, Russia, and the Philippines.\nL285@P9: Consumption in 2025/26 is revised 2 percent lower to 176.4 million tons, with weaker consumption\nL286@P9: in China, Brazil, the UAE, Russia, Belarus, Libya, Algeria, and the European Union more than\nL287@P9: offsetting larger estimates for Thailand, Sudan, and the Philippines.\nL288@P9: Exports in 2025/26 are revised up 3 percent to 64.7 million tons, on higher shipments from the\nL289@P9: European Union, Russia, Belarus, Guatemala, and Thailand overcoming lower shipments from\nL290@P9: Pakistan.\nL291@P9: Imports in 2025/26 are revised down 1 percent to 57.6 million tons, primarily reflecting a large\nL292@P9: decrease in imports by China, but also reductions for the UAE, Iran, Libya, and Bangladesh. Imports\nL293@P9: for India, the United States, Russia, and the Philippines are revised higher.\nL294@P9: Ending stocks in 2025/26 are revised up 4 percent to 45.0 million tons, on account of build-ups in\nL295@P9: India, the European Union, and China which largely offset a drawdown in Thailand.\nL296@P9: Revisions to 2025/26 Global Sugar Estimates\nL297@P9: 0\nL298@P9: 10\nL299@P9: 20\nL300@P9: 30\nL301@P9: 40\nL302@P9: 50\nL303@P9: 60\nL304@P9: 70\nL305@P9: Exports Imports Ending\nL306@P9: Stocks\nL307@P9: Million Tons\nL308@P9: Global Sugar Trade and\nL309@P9: Ending Stocks\nL310@P9: 2025/26 (May) 2025/26 (Nov)\nL311@P9: 100\nL312@P9: 115\nL313@P9: 130\nL314@P9: 145\nL315@P9: 160\nL316@P9: 175\nL317@P9: 190\nL318@P9: Production Consumption\nL319@P9: Million Tons\nL320@P9: Global Sugar Production and\nL321@P9: Consumption\nL322@P9: 2025/26 (May) 2025/26 (Nov)\nL323@P9: Foreign Agricultural Service/USDA 10 November 2026\nL324@P9-10: Global Market AnalysisSelect Revisions to 2025/26 Forecasts\nL325@P10: Production:\nL326@P10: May Nov Change Reason\nL327@P10: WORLD 186,056 187,118 1,062\nL328@P10: Brazil 43,800 43,295 -505 Greater use for ethanol\nL329@P10: production\nL330@P10: EU-27 15,519 16,258 739 Stronger beet sugar recovery\nL331@P10: China 12,600 12,950 350 Larger sugarcane area\nL332@P10: Thailand 11,258 11,528 270 Better sugarcane yields\nL333@P10: Guatemala 2,681 2,898 217 Larger sugarcane area\nL334@P10: Exports:\nL335@P10: May Nov Change Reason\nL336@P10: WORLD 62,648 64,756 2,108\nL337@P10: EU-27 1,258 1,988 730 Larger refined sugar exports to\nL338@P10: key partners\nL339@P10: Russia 551 1,000 449 Strong refined sugar trade with\nL340@P10: Central Asia\nL341@P10: Guatemala 1,608 1,840 232 Larger raw exports to North\nL342@P10: America and refined to Africa\nL343@P10: Thailand 6,000 6,200 200\nL344@P10: Steady growth in trade with\nL345@P10: Indonesia, price sensitive\nL346@P10: importers\nL347@P10: Imports:\nL348@P10: May Nov Change Reason\nL349@P10: WORLD 58,332 57,637 -695\nL350@P10: China 5,450 4,296 -1,154 